// Practice quiz: Selecting and changing the DOM
// First draft: unreviewed. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: html-css-js/dom-tooling-and-async/selecting-and-changing-the-dom

::o1 which returns null::What does querySelectorAll return when nothing matches? {
=An empty NodeList #Correct. Its length is 0 and looping over it does nothing.
~null #Not quite. That is what querySelector returns.
~undefined #Not quite. It always returns a list.
~It throws an error #Not quite. An unmatched selector is not an error.
}

::o1 first match::Three elements have class row. What does document.querySelector(".row") return? {
=The first of them in document order #Correct. querySelector returns only the first match.
~The last of them #Not quite. Matching runs in document order and stops at the first.
~A list of all three #Not quite. That is querySelectorAll.
~null, because the match is ambiguous #Not quite. Several matches are fine.
}

::o2 keeping classes::An element has classes card and open. Why prefer classList.add("wide") to setting className to wide? {
=classList.add keeps the existing classes #Correct. Setting className replaces them all.
~className cannot be assigned #Not quite. It can, but it replaces the whole value.
~classList.add also changes the CSS file #Not quite. It only changes the element.
~There is no difference #Not quite. className would lose card and open.
}

::o2 created but missing::You call document.createElement("li") and set its text, but nothing appears. Why? {
=It has not been attached to the document yet #Correct. A created node stays off the page until you attach it.
~li elements need a class #Not quite. A class is not required for display.
~textContent only works after attaching #Not quite. Text can be set at any time.
~createElement copies an existing element #Not quite. It creates a new, unattached one.
}

::o3 order of changes::A list holds A, B. Code runs list.prepend(C) and then list.lastElementChild.remove(), where C is a new item. What does the list hold? {
=C, A #Correct. C goes first, then the last item B is removed.
~A, B #Not quite. Both changes do take effect.
~C, B #Not quite. B is the last item, so it is the one removed.
~A, C #Not quite. prepend puts C at the start.
}
